<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torrijos–Carter Treaties</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Torrijos–Carter Treaties were two agreements signed on 7 September 1977 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>'s leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-torrijos">Omar Torrijos</a> and US President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-carter">Jimmy Carter</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> Treaty abolished the Canal Zone in 1979 and provided for joint administration until the canal passed fully to Panama on 31 December 1999, while the Neutrality Treaty guaranteed the canal's permanent neutrality. They were approved by a Panamanian plebiscite and narrowly ratified by the US Senate in 1978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torrijos-carter-treaties.jpg" alt="Torrijos–Carter Treaties" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: White House photo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jimmy_Carter_and_General_Omar_Torrijos_signing_the_Panama_Canal_Treaty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> had sought to renegotiate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hay-bunau-varilla-treaty">Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty</a> almost since it was signed in November 1903, weeks after independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>. Pressure grew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a>. On 9 January 1964 students raising the Panamanian flag in the Canal Zone were harassed and the flag was torn; in the rioting that followed over 20 Panamanians were killed and about 500 injured, mostly by fire from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> troops. The day is now Martyrs' Day in Panama, and Panama broke off relations until talks on a new treaty were agreed.</p><p>A treaty negotiated in 1967 was never ratified in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>. After the 1968 military coup the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-torrijos">Omar Torrijos</a> rejected it, held a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> session in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-city">Panama City</a> in March 1973 and won the 1974 Tack-Kissinger agreement, which framed later negotiations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Carter administration made the canal a priority, guided by a commission under Ambassador Sol Linowitz. Talks resumed on 15 February 1977 and ended on 10 August. Ellsworth Bunker and Linowitz negotiated for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, Rómulo Escobar Bethancourt for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>. The Neutrality Treaty gave the United States a permanent right to defend the canal's neutral service, and the Panama Canal Treaty set the handover of operations and primary defence.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, where press and party restrictions were lifted for the campaign, the treaties won 67.4% in a referendum on 23 October 1977, with a turnout of 96%. The Senate gave its consent by 68 votes to 32 on 16 March and 18 April 1978, after an amendment sponsored by Senator Dennis DeConcini.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Conservatives led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a>, Strom Thurmond and Jesse Helms called the treaties the surrender of a strategic asset, while supporters ranged from the Joint Chiefs of Staff and Howard Baker to William F. Buckley Jr., who debated Reagan on Firing Line. Under the treaty some 7,000 buildings and nearly 370,000 acres passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> by 2000, many areas in stages as enclaves within Panamanian territory. In December 2024 President-elect Donald Trump raised the idea of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> taking the canal back, which Panama's president José Raúl Mulino rejected.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Torrijos–Carter Treaties” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Omar Torrijos then ruled as ‘Maximum Leader’ until his death in a plane crash in 1981, combining authoritarian control with land reform, labour legislation, expanded public services and the <b>Torrijos–Carter Treaties</b> of 1977.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Torrijos-Carter Treaties</b> of 1977 abolished the Canal Zone on 1 October 1979, returning most of its territory to the Republic of Panama.</blockquote><a href="/state/panama-canal-zone">Panama Canal Zone</a></li></ul></section>
